ts before uppercase letters before lowercase letters).\n2. Swap the case of every letter (lowercase becomes uppercase and uppercase becomes lowercase); leave digits unchanged.\n3. Reverse the string.\n4. Keep only the characters at odd positions (the 1st, 3rd, 5th, ‚Ä¶, counting from 1).\n\nWhat is the resulting string?\n\nGive the final string exactly, and nothing else after ANSWER:.\nReason as much as you like, then finish with one final line of the form\nANSWER: <your answer>"},{"family":"strings","id":7,"prompt":"Start with the string (between the brackets, without them): [cv8mR34la80VSvk]\n\nApply these steps in order:\n\n1. Shift every letter forward by 9 places in the alphabet, wrapping from z back to a (and Z back to A); keep each letter's case; leave digits unchanged.\n2. Rotate the string left by 3: move its first 3 characters to the end, in the same order.\n3. Reverse the string.\n4. Keep only the characters at odd positions (the 1st, 3rd, 5th, ‚Ä¶, counting from 1).\n\nWhat is the resulting string?\n\nGive the final string exactly, and nothing else after ANSWER:.\nReason as much as you like, then finish with one final line of the form\nANSWER: <your answer>"},{"family":"spec","id":8,"prompt":"Write a JavaScript function f(xs), where xs is an array of integers, that returns an integer computed as follows:\n\n1. Keep every element.\n2. Replace each kept element x with 1 * x - 1.\n3. Return the sum of the resulting elements (0 if there are none).\n\nReply with only the function, in a single ```js code block. Name it f, take one parameter xs, use no libraries, and write no import, require or export statements."},{"family":"order","id":9,"prompt":"Facts:\n\nThe Juniper file was registered on 17 February 1966.\nThe Birch file was registered on 7 November 1903.\nThe Dogwood file was registered on 7 April 1995.\nThe Fir file was registered on 13 April 2010.\nThe Ginkgo file was registered on 4 April 1934.\nThe Hazel file was registered on 15 October 1962.\nThe Ivy file was registered on 22 October 1933.\n\nList the 7 labels from the latest registration to the earliest.\n\nGive the labels only, in the requested order, separated by commas.\nReason as much as you like, then finish with one final line of the form\nANSWER: <your answer>"},{"family":"order","id":10,"prompt":"Facts:\n\nThe Hazel file was registered on 28 December 1965.\nThe Ginkgo file was registered on 1 June 1904.\nThe Dogwood file was registered on 16 August 2018.\nThe Fir file was registered on 9 July 1952.\nThe Elm file was registered on 24 June 1912.\nThe Juniper file was registered on 1 August 2024.\n\nList the 6 labels from the earliest registration to the latest.\n\nGive the labels only, in the requested order, separated by commas.\nReason as much as you like, then finish with one final line of the form\nANSWER: <your answer>"},{"family":"spec","id":11,"prompt":"Write a JavaScript function f(xs), where xs is an array of integers, that returns an integer computed as follows:\n\n1. Keep only the elements less than 6.\n2. Leave the kept elements unchanged.\n3. Return the largest resulting element, or -4 if there are none.\n\nReply with only the function, in a single ```js code block. Name it f, take one parameter xs, use no libraries, and write no import, require or export statements."},{"family":"spec","id":12,"prompt":"Write a JavaScript function f(xs), where xs is an array of integers, that returns an integer computed as follows:\n\n1. Keep only the elements greater than -9.\n2. Replace each kept element with its absolute value.\n3. Return the alternating sum of the resulting elements: the first minus the second plus the third minus the fourth, and so on (0 if there are none).\n\nReply with only the function, in a single ```js code block. Name it f, take one parameter xs, use no libraries, and write no import, require or export statements."},{"family":"logic","id":13,"prompt":"5 people (Esme, Fitz, Gwen, Dev, Bram) sit in a row of 5 seats numbered 1 to 5 from left to right. All of the following are true:\n\n1. Esme sits in seat 2.\n2. Bram and Esme sit next to each other.\n3. Fitz sits in seat 5.\n4. Bram sits immediately to the left of Dev.\n5. There are exactly 0 seats between Esme and Bram.\n\nWho sits where?\n\nGive the names in seat order from seat 1 to the last seat, separated by commas.\nReason as much as you like, then finish with one final line of the form\nANSWER: <your answer>"},{"family":"logic","id":14,"prompt":"4 people (Dev, Esme, Ada, Bram) sit in a row of 4 seats numbered 1 to 4 from left to right. All of the following are true:\n\n1. Dev sits in seat 3.\n2. Dev does not sit in seat 2.\n3. Esme does not sit in seat 1.\n4. Esme sits at one end of the row.\n5. There are exactly 2 seats between Esme and Bram.\n6. Bram sits in seat 1.\n7. Bram sits at one end of the row.\n\nWho sits where?\n\nGive the names in seat order from seat 1 to the last seat, separated by commas.\nReason as much as you like, then finish with one final line of the form\nANSWER: <your answer>"},{"family":"logic","id":15,"prompt":"5 people (Dev, Fitz, Cleo, Esme, Gwen) sit in a row of 5 seats numbered 1 to 5 from left to right. All of the following are true:\n\n1. Esme does not sit at either end of the row.\n2. There are exactly 3 seats between Gwen and Cleo.\n3. Fitz sits somewhere to the left of Gwen.\n4. Esme and Cleo do not sit next to each other.\n5. There are exactly 0 seats between Gwen and Dev.\n\nWho sits where?\n\nGive the names in seat order from seat 1 to the last seat, separated by commas.\nReason as much as you like, then finish with one final line of the form\nANSWER: <your answer>"},{"family":"logic","id":16,"prompt":"5 people (Cleo, Esme, Fitz, Gwen, Dev) sit in a row of 5 seats numbered 1 to 5 from left to right. All of the following are true:\n\n1. Cleo sits in seat 2.\n2. Fitz sits in seat 5.\n3. Cleo does not sit at either end of the row.\n4. Esme sits at one end of the row.\n5. Dev sits immediately to the left of Fitz.\n\nWho sits where?\n\nGive the names in seat order from seat 1 to the last seat, separated by commas.\nReason as much as you like, then finish with one final line of the form\nANSWER: <your answer>"},{"family":"strings","id":17,"prompt":"Start with the string (between the brackets, without them): [u06jwfsscQvXtTfjGT2]\n\nApply these steps in order:\n\n1. Rotate the string left by 7: move its first 7 characters to the end, in the same order.\n2. Keep only the characters at odd positions (the 1st, 3rd, 5th, ‚Ä¶, counting from 1).\n3. Swap the case of every letter (lowercase becomes uppercase and uppercase becomes lowercase); leave digits unchanged.\n\nWhat is the resulting string?\n\nGive the final string exactly, and nothing else after ANSWER:.\nReason as much as you like, then finish with one final line of the form\nANSWER: <your answer>"},{"family":"order","id":18,"prompt":"Facts:\n\nThe Ivy file was registered on 15 February 1962.\nThe Fir file was registered on 14 October 1946.\nThe Hazel file was registered on 16 April 2005.\nThe Alder file was registered on 19 April 1922.\nThe Elm file was registered on 2 August 1965.\nThe Ginkgo file was registered on 2 August 2005.\n\nList the 6 labels from the latest registration to the earliest.\n\nGive the labels only, in the requested order, separated by commas.\nReason as much as you like, then finish with one final line of the form\nANSWER: <your answer>"},{"family":"order","id":19,"prompt":"Facts:\n\nThe Fir crate weighs 2,418 kg.\nThe Dogwood crate weighs 83,766 kg.\nThe Ginkgo crate weighs 28,564 kg.\nThe Ivy crate weighs 66,430 kg.\nThe Alder crate weighs 36,873 kg.\nThe Cedar crate weighs 12,774 kg.\nThe Elm crate weighs 92,601 kg.\n\nList the 7 labels from the heaviest crate to the lightest.\n\nGive the labels only, in the requested order, separated by commas.\nReason as much as you like, then finish with one final line of the form\nANSWER: <your answer>"},{"family":"spec","id":20,"prompt":"Write a Java